Onan the conqueror, having become fabulously wealthy in his mid life
career of making large rocks into small rocks into smaller rocks,
became a demigod with 13,100,292 points.  High and low points of the
biography follow, along with various and sundry spoilers and comments. 

The mines were easy, since there were no polymorph traps in the mines.
Onan got the luckstone, and, since the shops were relatively useless,
started a long business relationship with the minetown priest (not
coaligned, but that doesn't matter) trading $ for protection (sounds
like a mob racket, doesn't it?).

Found an altar on level 12, which was promptly converted and used to
max out the luck.  While praying for food, Onan became Envoy of
Balance, which occasioned a mad dash back to the last location I
remembered seeing food rations.  Next time I'll carry the silly things
even when praying for food.

First wish was a throne wish on level 18 (GDSM).  Since a polymorh
trap had earlier made Onan a "new man" (and happily added two levels,
12 to 14), it was quest time.  Magic resistance made Thoth Amon a
joke.  Obtained bell and Heart of Ahriman.

The closest call was on the Medusa level, where I thought I was
tinning a corpse and hit 'y' to confirm.  This put Onan into a pool of
water, but fortunately he didn't drown.

By the time Onan got the castle wand (only one charge), he had
basically everything except intrinsic teleport control --- the idea of
accumulating tins of tengu to increase the chance of getting teleport
control did not work.  Polypiling to get a ring of teleport control
did work, fortunately.  Later on, multiple reverse genocides of tengu
still didn't work, so this was abondened as a waste of magic markers.
Speaking of genocide, the only things which were genocided were Ls
(first scroll of genocide, which was blessed), and, much later,
cockatrices, in order to avoid being killed by a soldier wielding a
cockatrice corpse.  Eventually, Onan wished for the platinum yendorian
express card.  Nice item.

Onan mapped hell completely, cleared out the wizard's tower (an amulet
of magical breathing was used to make fighting the moat monsters
risk-free, since a &^%^% kraken had drowned my last good character)
except for the wizard, since I wanted to have everything ready for a
quick and painless endgame.  Eventually everything was in place,
including, amusingly enough an intrinsic AC of -38, which became an AC
of -86 when wearing armor.  This ridicuous AC was obtained by hauling
every scap of $ in the dungeon to the minetown priest, converting that
altar, killing the priest, then giving all the gold to the priest of
Moloch in the valley of the dead.  A pity there weren't any randomly
generated temples this game, or Onan's AC would have been below -100. 

Offed the wizard and opened up the sanctum.  Killing off the priests
was easy enough, and a wand of cancellation made the return of the
wizard trivial enough to be ignored, until he stole the heart of
Ahriman.  Then he had to be killed again.  A pity cancellation doesn't
remove the wizards ability to steal stuff.  Getting out of hell was
more painful than it should have been, since I didn't use a helm of
opposite alignment to make Onan chaotic.  Also, once I was in the
regular dungeon levels, the trick of throwing a potion of paralysis at
the wizard while standing on the up staircase didn't work properly,
although I could only try it twice since that's how many potions of
paralysis I had.  Maybe hitting the wizard with slow monster first
would increase the chance of it working.

The elemental levels were easy, since Onan had scrolls of gold
detection and potions of confusion.  When he arrrived at the Astral
plane, Onan had enough spare food rations that he could afford to take
his time and kill off all the hostiles.  The "demigods" really are
rather feeble, although they are the strongest hostiles there.  At any
rate, when all the action had died down, Onan gave the amulet to crom
and ascended.

Some random comments (all, IMHO, of course):

The scoring system is a little strange.  Gems in any normal quantity
are completely irrelevant to one's score, so why bother counting them
at all.  I deliberately polypiled rocks into gems to an absurd degree
to see what a sensible upper limit on the gem part of the score might
be (in this game it was around 5,800,000).

High level characters have things too easy now.  The quest artifacts
should not be wishable, and they should be made more equal in terms of
usefulness (i.e. the yendorian express card is too useful, and the
magic mirror of merlin is pretty feeble).  Perhaps all wands should be
rechargeable only one time.  Genocide needs to be reduced in
usefulness as well.  Either eliminate scrolls of genocide, or make a
relatively large class of unpleasant monsters, including Ls,
ungenocideable.  Perhaps the ranks of the demons could be increased
with L lookalikes which are not genocideable (being demons).  Conflict
also needs to have limits put on it --- perhaps if the wizard is
present (like if he's summoned pets to kill the thief) conflict would
not work.  Also, if using conflict to get through the Astral plane is
to be discouraged, stronger measures than a hostile angle or three are
needed.  Finally, the bell of opening needs to be changed --- either
summon lots of pit fiends, balrogs and nalfeshnees along with the
undead, or skip the summoning entirely.

Most of the hazards high level characters face are death traps which
can be avoided only if one has the proper items and/or knows the
danger involved.  Examples are being drowned by a kraken, bashed with
a cockatrice corpse and killed by the touch of death.  Low level
characters simply get killed by monsters.  So maybe there should be
monsters that are a legitimate threat to high level characters,
although that's difficult to arrange.  One possibility is to have the
wizard (and some other high level monsters) create a hostile duplicate
of the player (the corpse and inventory of this duplicate would, of
course, dissipate into clouds of smoke if the player succeeds in
dispatching the duplicate).  Of course named weapons would be included
in this duplication.  "Your duplicate wields the blessed +7 Tsurugi of
Muramasa!"  Another change which would make the lives of high level
characters more interesting is a tengu like creature with more hit
points and an annoying habit of stealing items.  Also, large boxes
with ridiculous quantities of items in them should have a chance of
becoming infested with magic eating worms, causing the player to
return to a worm-eaten empty box, which crumbles to dust at a touch.

Low level characters need some help, I think.  One helpful thing would
be to have polymorph traps work only once for monsters, or if the
current situation is continued (where a dozen gnomes in the mines can
deliberately go onto a polymorph trap until they all become nasty high
level critters), make polymorph traps polymorph monsters (and players)
into random creatures within a few levels of the player's or monster's
original level.  Another change is that the bell and candelabrum
should be indestructible, like the book of the dead.  Having a rock
mole eat the bell is a rather ridiculous way to be permanently
screwed. 

Finally, I'd like to congratulate the dev team for making a wonderful
game.  Now back to work...

